   "etymology": "From Old Albanian ȳll (/yːɫ/), from Proto-Albanian *usla, from either\n* Proto-Indo-European *h₁us-los (“the burning one”) (compare Old English ysle (“hot ashes”)), from *h₁ews- (“to burn”) (compare Latin ūrere, Ancient Greek εὕω (heúō, “to singe”)); or\n* from Proto-Indo-European *h₂us-los (“the glowing one”) (compare Sanskrit उ॒स्र (usrá, “reddish, ruddy; bright; matutinal”)), from *h₂ews- (“to dawn”) (compare English Easter, Latin aurōra, and Lithuanian aušrà).",
